# OmniLens

AI-Powered Everyday Visual Assistance

Tagline: Understand your world. Through sound.

A camera-based assistant that interprets supported visual information, responds to spoken requests, and gives useful spoken guidance to blind and low-vision users.

See

OpenCV 5 processing, object detection, OCR and image analysis.

Understand

Interpret results, confidence, position and uncertainty.

Listen

Accept spoken commands without forcing the user to navigate the screen.

Speak

Deliver concise results, instructions, warnings and recovery messages.

## 1. First, understand exactly what we are building

OmniLens should not be just an ordinary object-detection website, a chatbot with a camera, or a collection of unrelated AI buttons.

It should behave as one continuous, voice-controlled visual assistant.

The user opens OmniLens, grants camera and microphone permission, and can speak naturally. The camera captures the visual scene. OpenCV 5 processes the relevant frame. The appropriate vision model or OCR component analyzes it. The application evaluates the result and speaks a response.

If the user asks it to find something that is not visible, OmniLens should not simply say “not found” and stop. It should decide what to do next, guide the user to adjust the camera, analyze another frame, and verify whether the requested object has appeared.

That last behaviour is especially important for our intended Agentic Vision approach.

### The complete experience

1\. Camera and microphone

Capture a frame and receive a voice command.

2\. OpenCV 5

Validate, decode, preprocess, crop and measure the image.

3\. Vision components

YOLO for supported objects; EasyOCR for text; suitable additional models for specialized tasks.

4\. Decision engine

Decide whether to answer, retry, request a new view, report uncertainty or stop.

5\. Spoken response

Give the result, then continue listening or analyzing as appropriate.

The key design rule is that each component has a clear responsibility. OpenCV does image processing; the detector detects objects; OCR extracts text; the decision engine controls the workflow; the voice controller interprets commands; the speech manager controls what gets spoken; and AWS runs a meaningful part of the actual vision workload.

We should retain your existing React/Vite frontend, Python/FastAPI backend, YOLO model, and EasyOCR integration wherever they are suitable. We should repair and extend the current project rather than rebuild everything without a reason.

