# 19. Testing: prove that every feature works

We should not finish implementation simply because the website opens or one example succeeds.

OmniLens needs unit tests, integration tests and end-to-end tests. We also need a documented evaluation dataset and measured results.

## 19.1 OpenCV pipeline tests

Test:

- Valid images.
- Empty input.
- Corrupted image bytes.
- Unsupported image format.
- Very large images.
- Unexpected colour channels.
- Different image dimensions.
- Low-contrast scenes.
- Blurry images.
- Cropped images.
- Coordinate transformations after resizing or cropping.

Check that invalid images fail safely and that valid images preserve the correct dimensions and coordinate mapping.

## 19.2 Object-detection tests

Test:

- Supported object clearly visible.
- Object partly occluded.
- Object far from the camera.
- Multiple instances of the same class.
- Multiple different classes.
- Cluttered backgrounds.
- Low light.
- An object that is not supported by the model.
- Model unavailable.
- Inference error.

Verify that class names, bounding boxes and confidence values are returned consistently.

## 19.3 Find Object tests

The active-search workflow needs its own test cases:

1. Target found in the first frame.
2. Target found after a new frame.
3. Target never found.
4. Target unsupported.
5. Frame invalid.
6. Model unavailable.
7. User cancels during search.
8. User gives a new target during search.
9. Search reaches the retry limit.
10. Search exceeds its timeout.
11. New frames are identical or stale.
12. Multiple candidate detections create ambiguity.

The key measure is not merely detection accuracy. It is also whether the complete task succeeds, terminates correctly and responds appropriately to failures.

## 19.4 OCR tests

Test:

- Clear printed text.
- Small text.
- Low-contrast text.
- Text at an angle.
- Text under uneven lighting.
- Curved packaging.
- Multiple text blocks.
- Multiple columns if that layout is supported.
- Blurry or partially hidden text.
- Images with no readable text.
- OCR initialization failure.

Measure character error rate or word error rate where ground-truth text is available.

For example, character error rate can be calculated as:

\\[ \mathrm{CER} = \frac{S+D+I}{N} \\]

where \\(S\\) is the number of character substitutions, \\(D\\) the deletions, \\(I\\) the insertions and \\(N\\) the number of characters in the reference text.

A lower CER indicates fewer character errors.

## 19.5 Live-detection tests

Verify:

- The camera starts and stops correctly.
- Frame processing is bounded.
- Results arrive in the correct order or stale results are discarded.
- Repeated announcements are suppressed.
- New commands work during live mode.
- Task changes do not require restarting the camera unnecessarily.
- Speech does not overlap.
- Stop cancels ongoing work.
- Network interruptions produce recoverable errors.
- The system does not exhaust memory during a sustained session.

## 19.6 Voice interaction tests

Test every supported command family, including variations in phrasing.

Measure:

- Command recognition success.
- Correct intent routing.
- Correct target extraction.
- Response latency.
- Behaviour when the command is ambiguous.
- Behaviour when the browser does not support speech recognition.
- Behaviour when microphone permission is denied.
- Whether the user can cancel promptly.
- Whether the application remains usable with keyboard and screen-reader controls.

Speech recognition can fail even when the application is correct, so we should distinguish recognition failures from incorrect command routing.

## 19.7 AWS integration tests

