- Model loading succeeds where the model is deployed.
- Resource usage stays within the configured limits.
- The service recovers from expected failures.
- No credentials or sensitive image content leak into logs.

A working local backend does not prove that the AWS deployment works, and a successful health check does not prove that the vision pipeline works.

# 20. Evaluation metrics: how we will prove OmniLens is useful

The competition judging criteria in your document place significant weight on technical execution, innovation, real-world impact, usability, documentation and cloud delivery.

Our evaluation should produce evidence for each area.

| Area                | What to measure                                                     | Why it matters                                                    |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Object detection    | Precision, recall and an appropriate detection metric such as mAP   | Shows whether supported objects are recognized correctly.         |
| OCR                 | Character error rate, word error rate or exact-text accuracy        | Measures the quality of extracted text.                           |
| Find Object         | Task success rate, retries, timeout rate and false success rate     | Measures the complete active-search workflow.                     |
| Scene description   | Groundedness and correctness against annotated scenes               | Checks whether summaries reflect actual visual evidence.          |
| Live detection      | Processing rate, end-to-end latency and duplicate-announcement rate | Measures real-time usability.                                     |
| Voice control       | Command recognition and intent-routing accuracy                     | Shows whether the user can control the assistant hands-free.      |
| Accessibility       | Task completion with keyboard/screen reader and usability feedback  | Checks whether the application is usable without visual reliance. |
| Reliability         | Request failures, recovery success and cancellation behaviour       | Demonstrates robust operation.                                    |
| AWS                 | Request latency, error rate, resource usage and reproducibility     | Demonstrates meaningful cloud delivery.                           |
| Privacy and control | Cancellation behaviour, retention policy and access-control tests   | Shows responsible operation.                                      |

### Important: no invented accuracy numbers

We should not claim “99% accuracy,” “real-time in all conditions” or “zero missed obstacles” unless we have measurements that support those statements.

The report should state:

- Number of test cases.
- How the test set was created.
- Which objects and conditions were represented.
- Which models and configurations were used.
- The actual measured results.
- Known failure cases.
- The limitations of the evaluation.

Even a small, carefully documented test set is better than a fabricated benchmark.

## 20.1 Compare OpenCV preprocessing fairly

For useful evidence, compare a baseline against the tested preprocessing pipeline using the same held-out test images.

For example:

- Baseline: original image.
- Variant: resized image with a specific tested contrast enhancement.
- Variant: perspective correction for an appropriate text-image subset.

Measure both recognition quality and processing time. If a transformation adds latency without improving results, we should not claim it as an improvement.

This also gives the technical report a clear explanation of why the selected OpenCV operations were retained.

## 20.2 Measure end-to-end latency

A detector's inference time is not the same as the time the user waits for a spoken result.

The total delay may include:

- Frame capture.
- Image encoding.
- Network transmission.
- Backend queueing.
- OpenCV processing.
- Model inference.
- Result serialization.
- Response transmission.
- Speech startup.

Measure the complete user-facing delay, alongside relevant component-level timings.

For live detection, record the distribution of delays across many requests rather than relying on one unusually fast sample.

