# 22. How to integrate everything without creating a giant, fragile application

The most important architecture principle is to build shared components and then compose the features from them.

Consider these examples:

- Describe and Find Object both need OpenCV and object detection.
- Read Text and Product Reader both need OpenCV, OCR and speech output.
- Live Detection needs camera management, OpenCV, object detection, duplicate suppression and speech scheduling.
- Obstacle Awareness needs object detection, spatial analysis and cautious wording.
- Currency Recognition needs its own validated model but can reuse camera, OpenCV and speech infrastructure.
- Person Presence can reuse object detection if the loaded model supports the required class.
- Find Object needs the decision engine, cancellation and task-state tracking.

The goal is not to build nine separate applications inside one website. It is to build one set of dependable shared capabilities that support multiple user-facing tasks.

## 22.1 Shared data structures

We should define common representations for:

- A captured frame.
- A detected object.
- A text region.
- A vision-processing result.
- A task status.
- A user command.
- A speech message.
- An execution-trace event.

Consistent structures reduce integration errors.

For example, if object detections use a standard bounding-box format, both Describe and Find Object can consume the same results. If every feature invents its own coordinate convention, spatial announcements become inconsistent.

## 22.2 Shared cancellation

Every long-running task should respect cancellation.

If the user says “Stop,” the application should:

1. Mark the active task as cancelled.
2. Prevent it from scheduling further work.
3. Cancel or ignore pending requests where possible.
4. Remove its queued speech messages.
5. Stop current speech when the user intends to stop the entire activity.
6. Prevent late responses from overwriting the new application state.
7. Release resources when appropriate.

Cancelling a browser request does not always mean that already-started server computation has stopped. The backend must check cancellation state between appropriate processing steps, and the frontend must ignore results from obsolete tasks.

## 22.3 Shared task identifiers

Every user request should have a task identifier.

This allows the frontend to know which result belongs to which request and helps prevent a slow earlier request from overwriting a newer result.

For example:

- Task A: “Find bottle.”
- Task B: “Read text.”
- Task A finishes after Task B starts.

If Task A is obsolete, its result should not unexpectedly replace the Read Text state or be spoken as the current result.

Task IDs, cancellation tokens or generation counters can help enforce this rule, depending on the existing architecture.

# 23. Failure handling: what should happen when things go wrong?

A reliable assistant must behave sensibly when the camera, model, network, OCR or speech system fails.

| Failure                            | Correct behaviour                                                                               |
| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Camera permission denied           | Explain the problem and offer a clear way to enable permission or use an available alternative. |
| No camera available                | Report that camera capture cannot start.                                                        |
| Invalid image                      | Reject it safely and request a usable frame.                                                    |
| YOLO model missing                 | Mark detection unavailable and return a clear error.                                            |
| OCR reader failed to initialize    | Disable or mark OCR unavailable rather than pretending it works.                                |
| Target not supported               | Explain that the current system cannot reliably search for that target.                         |
| Target not found                   | Request a new view when appropriate; stop after the retry budget.                               |
| Network failure                    | Explain that the vision service is unavailable and allow a safe retry.                          |
| AWS timeout                        | End or retry according to a bounded policy; do not loop indefinitely.                           |
| Speech recognition unavailable     | Provide a usable fallback and explain the limitation.                                           |
| Speech playback fails              | Show an accessible text result and allow another attempt.                                       |
| User cancels                       | Stop scheduling work and suppress obsolete announcements.                                       |
| Two commands arrive close together | Apply the documented task-priority and cancellation rules.                                      |
| Camera session ends unexpectedly   | Stop processing and report the issue.                                                           |
| Model returns uncertain detections | Communicate uncertainty rather than inventing certainty.                                        |

These are not optional polish items. They are part of the behaviour that makes OmniLens dependable.

# 24. Privacy, accessibility and responsible operation

The application is designed for blind and low-vision users, and its camera may capture people, private documents, home interiors or other sensitive visual information.

The project should therefore have a clear privacy and safety design.

## 24.1 Camera and microphone permissions

The application should explain why camera and microphone access are needed.

It should not imply that the camera or microphone is active when it is not. It should release camera resources when the session ends and make active-session status accessible.

## 24.2 Image and audio retention

The default should be to process images transiently and avoid storing raw camera frames or audio unnecessarily.

If the AWS service needs temporary processing storage, that should be controlled and documented. Logs should focus on request metadata, processing time, task status and errors rather than raw image content.

If audio is sent to an external speech-recognition service, the user should be told about that data flow.

## 24.3 User control

The user should be able to stop or cancel the task without navigating through multiple screens.

The system must not keep performing a search simply because the agent believes more attempts could help.

## 24.4 Honest claims

We should describe OmniLens as an assistive visual-information tool, not a guaranteed navigation or emergency-safety system.

It must not promise perfect object recognition, complete OCR accuracy or guaranteed obstacle avoidance.

For the final report, include the known limitations and explain the intended role of the system clearly.

